# First HONEST relayed reading, two machines on different networks
# 2026-09-10 04:5x UTC. RuggedFox (Fedora, 69.181.167.209) pulling from
# a MacBook tethered to a phone hotspot (172.20.10.5, public 71.134.157.175).
#
# THE GATE PASSED — this is the bench's OWN path line, not the daemon's:
#
#   path=relayed direct=[] relay=[https://usw1-1.relay.n0.iroh.link./]
#
# `direct=[]` is empty, and the class is `relayed`, not `mixed`. This is the
# reading `relay-leg-CONTAMINATED.stdout` failed to take.
#
# Different networks CONFIRMED by different public IPs (71.134.157.175 vs
# 69.181.167.209), not assumed from subnets.

pathprobe viewer=0 status=206 clen=Some(33554432) crange=Some("bytes 0-33554431/1073741824") bytes=33554432 secs=31.01 rate=8.7 Mbit/s ttfb=189.8ms gaps: n=17957 p50=0.1 p99=82.5 max=98.2ms stalls>500ms=0 >1s=0 >2s=0 worst_1s=7.9 Mbit/s verify=off

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS A PROBE, NOT THE BAR. Two reasons it does not close the NO-GO:
#
# 1. n=1 at 31 s. The bar is >=3 runs of 600 s reported as a distribution
#    (ARCH 18.5 — a single-run number is not a result).
#
# 2. AN UNEXCLUDED CONFOUND, which matters more. The SENDER is a laptop
#    tethered to a phone. Cellular uplink is typically 5-20 Mbit/s, so 8.7
#    may be the phone's upstream rather than the n0 relay. The bar's intent
#    is to characterise THE RELAY as the floor; this configuration cannot
#    separate the two.
#
#    The contaminated run reported 7.8 Mbit/s and this one 8.7. They agree —
#    but agreement between two numbers that may share a confound is not
#    corroboration, and 7.8 already sat inside the prior WORK_PLANE.md writes
#    down. That is the trap note 04b80710 named: a number that agrees with
#    your prior is the one nobody checks.
#
# TO DISAMBIGUATE, cheapest first:
#   - measure the phone's raw uplink (any speed test on the tethered Mac);
#     if it is ~9 Mbit/s, this reading is the uplink and says nothing about
#     the relay.
#   - or re-run with the ROLES REVERSED (RuggedFox serves on wired/home,
#     the Mac pulls) so the sender is not the cellular leg.
#   - or re-run relayed between two machines that both have real uplinks,
#     forcing relay by blocking direct rather than by NAT topology.

# ===========================================================================
# REVERSED LEG, same session — RuggedFox (wired) SERVES, Mac (cellular) PULLS.
# VERDICT: DISCARD. Not a relayed reading.
#
#   relay=[https://usw1-1.relay.n0.iroh.link./]
#   path=mixed direct=[69.181.167.209:47800]
#
# reversed-probe viewer=0 status=206 bytes=33554432 secs=8.11 rate=33.1 Mbit/s
#   ttfb=211.1ms gaps: n=6111 p50=0.4 p99=11.3 max=256.6ms
#   stalls>500ms=0 >1s=0 >2s=0 worst_1s=26.1 Mbit/s
#
# iroh HOLE-PUNCHED to the public IP across cellular. `direct=[…]` is
# non-empty, so by this bar's own rule the run is discarded, not averaged.
#
# WHAT IT DOES SETTLE: the 8.7 Mbit/s above WAS the phone's uplink, not the
# relay — reversing the sender moved it to 33.1. Two readings, two different
# bottlenecks, neither of them the relay.
#
# WHAT IT IS A REAL RESULT FOR, and it is not this bar: the DIRECT path
# between two machines on genuinely different networks (cellular <-> home
# broadband, different public IPs) sustains 33.1 Mbit/s with worst_1s 26.1
# and no stall over 257 ms. That clears 25 Mbit/s — for the hole-punched
# case, which WORK_PLANE.md explicitly calls "the best case, not the floor".
#
# DISCARD COUNT SO FAR: 2 (relay-leg-CONTAMINATED, and this one).
# KEPT RELAYED RUNS: 1, and it is confounded. The bar remains COULD-NOT-JUDGE.
#
# NEXT, to isolate the relay with a non-cellular sender: keep RuggedFox
# serving and BLOCK the direct path so iroh has only the relay — on the Mac,
# block outbound UDP to 69.181.167.209:47800 (and to 100.115.12.21 /
# 192.168.1.13 if Tailscale or the LAN is reachable). Then re-run and confirm
# `direct=[]` BEFORE trusting the rate. Only then take 3 x 600 s.

# ===========================================================================
# RE-READ, operator direction, same session: THE BAR WAS A PROXY AND THE
# THING IT PROXIED FOR PASSED.
#
# The pre-registered bar asked for >= 25 Mbit/s RELAYED because the relay is
# the floor WHEN HOLE-PUNCHING FAILS. Today it did not fail. Leg 2 punched
# through between a laptop on carrier CGNAT and a machine behind home NAT —
# different public IPs, no VPN, no port forward, no manual config — and
# carried 33.1 Mbit/s, ttfb 211 ms, worst_1s 26.1, no stall over 257 ms.
#
# 1080p wants roughly 8-15 Mbit/s. So the end-user claim the bar stood in for
# — "direct playback of a remote 1080p title is honest" — IS DEMONSTRATED, on
# the path most users get, in about the most hostile common NAT pairing there
# is.
#
# THE RELAY NUMBER IS NOT RETIRED, ITS JOB CHANGES. It sizes the RESIDUAL
# population whose hole-punch genuinely fails. That is a smaller question than
# the one the NO-GO was written to gate, and it should not hold the demo.
#
# WHAT IS STILL NOT SHOWN, and it is not the bar: this was 8.1 s and 32 MiB.
# "It connects and streams fast" is demonstrated. "It sustains a two-hour
# film" is not. The 3 x 600 s soak still earns its keep — now on the DIRECT
# path, which is the one that matters.
#
# The discipline that produced the discards is kept: every reading still
# states its own path= class, discards are still counted, and this re-read
# does not reclassify leg 2 as relayed. It says the relayed question was the
# wrong gate, not that the reading was something other than direct.
